  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "tNEPLbY1ZxIgaDKg47fC6liRnJ8=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:33:48.008Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "## URL derivation\n\nThe websocket endpoint is derived from the client's `url`: `http` becomes `ws`, `https` becomes `wss`, and `/subscriptions` is appended. For a Switchboard on `http://localhost:4001/graphql` that is `ws://localhost:4001/graphql/subscriptions`. There is **one socket server for the whole Switchboard**, not one per subgraph. Override it with the `subscriptionsUrl` prop, or set `realtime={false}` where there is no websocket to talk to.\n\nThe first `subscribe` opens **one** websocket for all subscribers and subscribes to `documentChanges` with no `search` argument. The server sends every change the connection may read, and the client applies each subscriber's filter on the way out. Because the `DocumentCache` the provider builds subscribes in its constructor, the socket opens as soon as the provider mounts.\n\n## Degradation, not dependency\n\nRealtime is an enhancement, never a dependency. A socket that cannot be opened or is refused is **logged once and then ignored**, and the app keeps working on the changes it makes itself \u2014 every successful `create`, `execute` and `deleteDocument` emits a local change event, which is what makes the cache invalidate after a dispatch with no websocket involved.\n\nThe failed socket is **closed rather than kept**, so the next component to subscribe tries again. But \u2014 and this is the trap \u2014 **nothing subscribes on sign-in of its own accord**. An app on a Switchboard that refuses anonymous subscribers and that authenticates *after* mount will have had its one subscribe attempt refused, and will then sit with no realtime indefinitely. The fix is to **remount the provider** once the user is in.\n\n## Socket auth\n\nAuth on the socket reuses the same `tokenProvider`, sent as the **lowercase `authorization` connection param**, and re-resolved on every reconnect. A Switchboard with auth enabled refuses a connection that arrives without it, so an anonymous subscriber gets no realtime there.\n\nOne cosmetic consequence of the dual event sources: own writes are announced twice \u2014 once locally, once by the server push \u2014 so the cache refetches twice. Harmless.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/08-ReactorBrowserClient/02-ApiReference.md and 01-OverviewAndQuickstart.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T14:33:47.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:33:48.008Z"
    }
  },
